IMPORT prelude
IMPORT `mva-dates`

-- Motor Vehicles Act 1959 (SA), Part 4 (third party insurance), with the Motor Vehicles (Third Party Insurance)
-- Regulations 2013 -- SEAT-NEUTRAL CORE, in the texts' own actors and terms: "the claimant", "the injured person", "the
-- insurer", "the insured person", "the nominal defendant", "the court". The claimant's questions are in mva-seat.l4 and
-- the insured owner's or driver's questions in mva-seat-insured.l4; both IMPORT this module and restate no rule.
--
-- PROVISIONS. Act: s 99(1) (bodily injury, participant, road race), (3), (3a), (4); s 99A(8), (10), (12), (17) with
-- s 109; s 104(2); ss 112, 113, 113A, 114; s 115(1), (3), (4); s 116(1)-(5), (7)-(7e); s 117 (as a note); s 118A(3);
-- s 118B (as a note); s 123; s 124(1)-(4), (6), (6a), (6b); s 124A(1)-(3); s 124AB; s 124AC; s 126; s 126A; s 127(2),
-- (6), (6a); s 127B(2)-(4); s 127C; s 133; s 134. Regulations: reg 4, reg 5, reg 6.
--
-- EDITION. Both texts are from the Open Australian Legal Corpus (South Australian legislation last collected
-- 2025-03-10), NOT the official texts, and may be out of date: the Act as at 2024-11-07, at
-- ../../registers/source-bundle/motor-vehicles-act-1959.txt; the Regulations as at 2021-11-01, at
-- ../../../motor-vehicles-third-party-insurance-regulations-2013/registers/source-bundle/
-- motor-vehicles-third-party-insurance-regulations-2013.txt (quoted here: the stable l4 cannot IMPORT across subjects).
--
-- COVERAGE. Accidents on or after 1 July 2013 (the day ss 126A, 127B and 127C and the Regulations began: Lifetime
-- Support Scheme Act 2013 Sch 2 cl 22(2), (4), (5)). An earlier accident answers `not covered`.
--
-- BASELINE ENCODING. Written with the writing-l4-rules skill, design defaults of 2026-10-18. No pipeline, no coverage
-- table, no independent test pass, no human gate. Not for public use as advice. See NOTES.md.
--
-- Conventions. Money in Australian dollars. Regulative clocks in days, from the day the duty starts.

§ `Part 4 -- third party insurance (seat-neutral core)`

§§ `Coverage`

`the first day of the 2013 scheme` MEANS `the date` 1 7 2013

GIVEN `the accident day` IS A DATE
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the accident is within this encoding` `the accident day` IF
    `the first day of the 2013 scheme` `is on or before` `the accident day`

§§ `s 99(1), (3), (3a), (4) -- death or bodily injury caused by or arising out of the use of a motor vehicle`

-- s 99(3): "death or bodily injury will be regarded as being caused by or arising out of the use of a motor vehicle only
-- if it is a direct consequence of -- (a) the driving of the vehicle; or (b) the vehicle running out of control; or (c) a
-- person travelling on a road colliding with the vehicle when the vehicle is stationary, or action taken to avoid such a
-- collision." (3a): "will not be regarded as ... if the death or bodily injury is caused by a terrorist act." (4): for a
-- conditionally registered (s 25) tractor, agricultural machine, mobile fork lift or self-propelled lawn care machine,
-- "only if it is caused by or arises out of the use of the vehicle (as referred to in subsection (3)) on a road".
-- s 99(1): "bodily injury includes mental or nervous shock". "terrorist act" is borrowed (Terrorism (Commonwealth
-- Powers) Act 2002): a fact. Fork F3: s 99(3) states a necessary condition ("only if"); this encoding reads it as the
-- test, so that meeting it (and (3a), (4)) answers yes.
DECLARE `How the death or injury came about` IS ONE OF
    `as a direct consequence of the driving of the vehicle`
    `as a direct consequence of the vehicle running out of control`
    `as a direct consequence of a person travelling on a road colliding with the vehicle while it was stationary, or acting to avoid such a collision`
    `in some other way`

DECLARE `The kind of vehicle` IS ONE OF
    `a conditionally registered tractor, agricultural machine, mobile fork lift or self-propelled lawn care machine`
    `any other motor vehicle`

DECLARE `Use of a motor vehicle answer` IS ONE OF
    `yes: it is caused by or arises out of the use of a motor vehicle (s 99(3))`
    `no: not a direct consequence of driving, running out of control, or a collision with the stationary vehicle (s 99(3))`
    `no: caused by a terrorist act (s 99(3a))`
    `no: a conditionally registered machine that was not on a road (s 99(4))`

GIVEN how IS A `How the death or injury came about`
      `it was a terrorist act` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the kind of vehicle` IS A `The kind of vehicle`
      `the vehicle was on a road` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A `Use of a motor vehicle answer`
`the use of a motor vehicle test (s 99(3), (3a), (4))` how `it was a terrorist act` `the kind of vehicle` `the vehicle was on a road` MEANS
    IF how EQUALS `in some other way`
    THEN `no: not a direct consequence of driving, running out of control, or a collision with the stationary vehicle (s 99(3))`
    ELSE IF `it was a terrorist act`
         THEN `no: caused by a terrorist act (s 99(3a))`
         ELSE IF     (`the kind of vehicle` EQUALS `a conditionally registered tractor, agricultural machine, mobile fork lift or self-propelled lawn care machine`)
                 AND (NOT `the vehicle was on a road`)
              THEN `no: a conditionally registered machine that was not on a road (s 99(4))`
              ELSE `yes: it is caused by or arises out of the use of a motor vehicle (s 99(3))`

GIVEN a IS A `Use of a motor vehicle answer`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `it is the use of a motor vehicle` a IF
    a EQUALS `yes: it is caused by or arises out of the use of a motor vehicle (s 99(3))`

§§ `s 99A(8), (10), (12), (17), s 109 -- whether a CTP policy was in force on the day of the accident`

-- s 99A(8): the policy "is in force ... as from the time at which the grant of registration, the exemption from
-- registration, or permit becomes effective and ... (a) remains in force for the whole of the period for which
-- registration is granted and for a further period of grace of 30 days (but falls due for renewal at the expiration of
-- the former period); or (b) remains ... in force for the whole of the period for which the exemption from registration,
-- or permit, is granted, but then expires." (10): "Where a policy of insurance comes into force ... during the period of
-- grace ..., the period of grace thereupon terminates" (the new policy then covers). (19): trade plates count as
-- registration. s 109: an unpaid or wrong premium "does not affect the validity or operation of the policy"; s 99A(17):
-- nor does "any error or omission on the part of the Registrar". Fork F2: the 30 days are counted from the day after the
-- last day of the registration period.
DECLARE `The vehicle's authority` IS ONE OF
    `registration (including trade plates)`
    `an exemption from registration`
    `a permit`

DECLARE `Policy in force answer` IS ONE OF
    `insured: the accident was within the period of the registration, exemption or permit (s 99A(8))`
    `insured: the accident was within the 30-day period of grace after the registration period ended (s 99A(8)(a))`
    `insured: under the policy that came into force with the renewal or new grant (s 99A(8), (10))`
    `not insured under this Part on that day: the policy had not yet come into force (s 99A(8))`
    `not insured under this Part on that day: the exemption or permit had ended (s 99A(8)(b))`
    `not insured under this Part on that day: the period of grace had ended (s 99A(8)(a))`

`the days of grace (s 99A(8)(a))` MEANS 30

GIVEN `the authority` IS A `The vehicle's authority`
      `the day it took effect` IS A DATE
      `the last day of its period` IS A DATE
      `a new grant or renewal took effect after that` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the day the new grant or renewal took effect` IS A DATE
      `the accident day` IS A DATE
GIVETH A `Policy in force answer`
`was a CTP policy in force (s 99A(8))` `the authority` `the day it took effect` `the last day of its period` `a new grant or renewal took effect after that` `the day the new grant or renewal took effect` `the accident day` MEANS
    IF NOT (`the day it took effect` `is on or before` `the accident day`)
    THEN `not insured under this Part on that day: the policy had not yet come into force (s 99A(8))`
    ELSE IF `the accident day` `is on or before` `the last day of its period`
         THEN `insured: the accident was within the period of the registration, exemption or permit (s 99A(8))`
         ELSE IF `a new grant or renewal took effect after that` AND (`the day the new grant or renewal took effect` `is on or before` `the accident day`)
              THEN `insured: under the policy that came into force with the renewal or new grant (s 99A(8), (10))`
              ELSE IF NOT (`the authority` EQUALS `registration (including trade plates)`)
                   THEN `not insured under this Part on that day: the exemption or permit had ended (s 99A(8)(b))`
                   ELSE IF `the accident day` `is on or before` (`the last day of its period` `plus days` `the days of grace (s 99A(8)(a))`)
                        THEN `insured: the accident was within the 30-day period of grace after the registration period ended (s 99A(8)(a))`
                        ELSE `not insured under this Part on that day: the period of grace had ended (s 99A(8)(a))`

§§ `ss 104(2), 115, 116(1)-(3), 118A -- who answers the claim`

-- s 116(1): "uninsured motor vehicle means a motor vehicle in relation to which no policy of insurance as required by
-- this Part is in force, but does not include" (a)-(d) vehicles under certain public liability or regulation-required
-- policies, and "(e) a motor vehicle in relation to which there is in force a policy of insurance -- (i) that complies with
-- the law of some other State or Territory ... and (ii) under which the owner and driver ... are insured against
-- liability ... arising out of the use of the motor vehicle in this State". s 115(1): "the identity of the vehicle has
-- not after due inquiry and search been ascertained" -- an action "against the nominal defendant". s 116(2): damages for
-- death or bodily injury "arising out of the use of an uninsured motor vehicle on a road" -- an action "against the
-- nominal defendant". s 118A(3): where the Governor has declared that s 118A applies to an approved insurer (wound up or
-- in a compromise with creditors, with insufficient assets), a claimant "may make or bring that claim or action or
-- enforce that judgment against the nominal defendant". Finding 1: s 115 has no "on a road" limit; s 116(2) does.
DECLARE `The vehicle's insurance` IS ONE OF
    `insured under a CTP policy under this Part`
    `insured under a policy complying with another State's or Territory's law that covers its use in South Australia`
    `covered by a public liability or other policy that s 116(1)(a)-(d) accepts`
    `not insured at all`
    `not identified after due inquiry and search`

DECLARE `Who answers the claim` IS ONE OF
    `the vehicle's CTP insurer: the claim is made against the insured owner or driver, and the insurer deals with it (ss 104, 125, 126A)`
    `the nominal defendant, in place of an approved insurer declared under s 118A (s 118A(3))`
    `the nominal defendant, because the vehicle was not identified (s 115)`
    `the nominal defendant, because the vehicle was uninsured (s 116)`
    `not under this Part: the other State's or Territory's CTP insurer, under that law (s 116(1)(e))`
    `not under this Part: the public liability or other insurer s 116(1)(a)-(d) names`
    `no claim against the nominal defendant: the uninsured vehicle was not on a road (s 116(2))`
    `no claim under this Part: the death or injury was not caused by and did not arise out of the use of a motor vehicle (s 99)`
    `not covered: an accident before 1 July 2013`

GIVEN `the accident day` IS A DATE
      `the use test` IS A `Use of a motor vehicle answer`
      `the insurance` IS A `The vehicle's insurance`
      `the uninsured vehicle was on a road` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the insurer has been declared under s 118A` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A `Who answers the claim`
`who answers the claim` `the accident day` `the use test` `the insurance` `the uninsured vehicle was on a road` `the insurer has been declared under s 118A` MEANS
    IF NOT (`the accident is within this encoding` `the accident day`)
    THEN `not covered: an accident before 1 July 2013`
    ELSE IF NOT (`it is the use of a motor vehicle` `the use test`)
         THEN `no claim under this Part: the death or injury was not caused by and did not arise out of the use of a motor vehicle (s 99)`
         ELSE CONSIDER `the insurance`
              WHEN `insured under a CTP policy under this Part` THEN
                  IF `the insurer has been declared under s 118A`
                  THEN `the nominal defendant, in place of an approved insurer declared under s 118A (s 118A(3))`
                  ELSE `the vehicle's CTP insurer: the claim is made against the insured owner or driver, and the insurer deals with it (ss 104, 125, 126A)`
              WHEN `insured under a policy complying with another State's or Territory's law that covers its use in South Australia` THEN `not under this Part: the other State's or Territory's CTP insurer, under that law (s 116(1)(e))`
              WHEN `covered by a public liability or other policy that s 116(1)(a)-(d) accepts` THEN `not under this Part: the public liability or other insurer s 116(1)(a)-(d) names`
              WHEN `not identified after due inquiry and search` THEN `the nominal defendant, because the vehicle was not identified (s 115)`
              WHEN `not insured at all` THEN
                  IF `the uninsured vehicle was on a road`
                  THEN `the nominal defendant, because the vehicle was uninsured (s 116)`
                  ELSE `no claim against the nominal defendant: the uninsured vehicle was not on a road (s 116(2))`

GIVEN w IS A `Who answers the claim`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the claim is against the nominal defendant` w IF
       w EQUALS `the nominal defendant, in place of an approved insurer declared under s 118A (s 118A(3))`
    OR w EQUALS `the nominal defendant, because the vehicle was not identified (s 115)`
    OR w EQUALS `the nominal defendant, because the vehicle was uninsured (s 116)`

GIVEN w IS A `Who answers the claim`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `there is a CTP claim under this Part` w IF
       `the claim is against the nominal defendant` w
    OR w EQUALS `the vehicle's CTP insurer: the claim is made against the insured owner or driver, and the insurer deals with it (ss 104, 125, 126A)`

-- s 104(2): "a policy of insurance complies with this Part even though it does not extend to liability arising from the
-- death of, or bodily injury to, a participant in a road race caused by the act or omission of another participant in
-- the road race". Whether the policy (in terms the CTP Regulator determines, not deposited) does extend to it is not in
-- the Act: an input would be needed, so the answer names the gap.
DECLARE `Road race answer` IS ONE OF
    `the Act requires the policy to cover it (s 104(1)(a))`
    `the Act does not require the policy to cover it; whether it does depends on the policy terms the CTP Regulator determines (s 104(2))`

GIVEN `I was a participant in a road race` IS A BOOLEAN, `another participant caused it` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A `Road race answer`
`the road race exception (s 104(2))` `I was a participant in a road race` `another participant caused it` MEANS
    IF `I was a participant in a road race` AND `another participant caused it`
    THEN `the Act does not require the policy to cover it; whether it does depends on the policy terms the CTP Regulator determines (s 104(2))`
    ELSE `the Act requires the policy to cover it (s 104(1)(a))`

-- s 116(3)(b): where an action lies against the nominal defendant under s 116(2), "no action for damages in respect of
-- the death or bodily injury may be commenced or proceeded with against" the person who would have been insured, "or a
-- person liable in respect of the acts or omissions of such a person".
GIVEN w IS A `Who answers the claim`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the uninsured owner or driver cannot be sued (s 116(3)(b))` w IF
    w EQUALS `the nominal defendant, because the vehicle was uninsured (s 116)`

§§ `s 126A, regs 4-6 -- the notice of claim`

-- s 126A(1): "A person who seeks to make a claim for damages or other compensation in relation to the death of, or bodily
-- injury to, a person for which insurance is provided under this Part must furnish the insurer or the nominal defendant
-- (as the case may be) with a notice of claim that complies with the requirements of subsection (2)." (2)(a): "must be
-- provided at a time or within a period prescribed by the regulations". Reg 4(1): "(a) in the case of a notice of a
-- claim in relation to an action against the nominal defendant -- as soon as reasonably practicable after it becomes
-- apparent that -- (i) the identity of the relevant motor vehicle is not readily ascertainable; or (ii) the relevant
-- motor vehicle was uninsured; or (b) in any other case -- within 6 months after the relevant motor vehicle accident."
-- (A claim against the nominal defendant under s 118A falls under reg 4(1)(b): it is not about an unidentified or
-- uninsured vehicle. Fork F4.)
`the months for the notice of claim (reg 4(1)(b))` MEANS 6

DECLARE `Notice deadline` IS ONE OF
    `within 6 months after the accident: on or before` HAS `the last day` IS A DATE
    `as soon as reasonably practicable after it became apparent that the vehicle could not be identified or was uninsured, which was on` HAS `the day it became apparent` IS A DATE
    `no notice under s 126A: there is no CTP claim under this Part`

GIVEN w IS A `Who answers the claim`, `the accident day` IS A DATE, `the day it became apparent` IS A DATE
GIVETH A `Notice deadline`
`the time for the notice of claim (s 126A(2)(a), reg 4(1))` w `the accident day` `the day it became apparent` MEANS
    IF     w EQUALS `the nominal defendant, because the vehicle was not identified (s 115)`
        OR w EQUALS `the nominal defendant, because the vehicle was uninsured (s 116)`
    THEN `as soon as reasonably practicable after it became apparent that the vehicle could not be identified or was uninsured, which was on` `the day it became apparent`
    ELSE IF `there is a CTP claim under this Part` w
         THEN `within 6 months after the accident: on or before` (`the accident day` `plus months` `the months for the notice of claim (reg 4(1)(b))`)
         ELSE `no notice under s 126A: there is no CTP claim under this Part`

-- s 126A(2)(b)-(d) and regs 5, 6: what the notice must contain or be accompanied by.
DECLARE `Notice item` IS ONE OF
    `the notice in the manner and form the Minister approves (s 126A(2)(b))`
    `a statement setting out details of the claim (s 126A(2)(c)(i))`
    `a medical practitioner's certificate or opinion of the nature and probable cause of the death or injury, in the form the CTP Regulator determines (s 126A(2)(c)(ii), reg 5(1))`
    `the police report number of any report made to a police officer under the Road Traffic Act 1961 about the accident (s 126A(2)(c)(iii))`
    `evidence of income or earning capacity, such as payslips, PAYG payment summaries or income tax returns (reg 5(2)(a))`
    `evidence of medical expenses incurred up to the date of the claim, such as accounts or receipts (reg 5(2)(b))`
    `the certificate of death (reg 5(2)(c))`
    `the claimant's driver's licence number (reg 5(2)(d))`
    `proof of the claimant's identity (reg 5(2)(e))`
    `a statement in the CTP Regulator's form authorising access to records and other information relevant to the claim (s 126A(2)(d), reg 6)`

GIVEN item IS A `Notice item`, b IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A LIST OF `Notice item`
`the item` item `if` b MEANS IF b THEN LIST item ELSE EMPTY

GIVEN `a police report was made` IS A BOOLEAN
      `it claims loss of income or earning capacity` IS A BOOLEAN
      `it claims medical expenses` IS A BOOLEAN
      `it is a death claim under Part 5 of the Civil Liability Act 1936` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the claimant was driving a motor vehicle when injured` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A LIST OF `Notice item`
`what the notice of claim must include (s 126A(2), regs 5, 6)` `a police report was made` `it claims loss of income or earning capacity` `it claims medical expenses` `it is a death claim under Part 5 of the Civil Liability Act 1936` `the claimant was driving a motor vehicle when injured` MEANS
    concat (LIST
        (LIST `the notice in the manner and form the Minister approves (s 126A(2)(b))`),
        (LIST `a statement setting out details of the claim (s 126A(2)(c)(i))`),
        (LIST `a medical practitioner's certificate or opinion of the nature and probable cause of the death or injury, in the form the CTP Regulator determines (s 126A(2)(c)(ii), reg 5(1))`),
        `the item` `the police report number of any report made to a police officer under the Road Traffic Act 1961 about the accident (s 126A(2)(c)(iii))` `if` `a police report was made`,
        `the item` `evidence of income or earning capacity, such as payslips, PAYG payment summaries or income tax returns (reg 5(2)(a))` `if` `it claims loss of income or earning capacity`,
        `the item` `evidence of medical expenses incurred up to the date of the claim, such as accounts or receipts (reg 5(2)(b))` `if` `it claims medical expenses`,
        `the item` `the certificate of death (reg 5(2)(c))` `if` `it is a death claim under Part 5 of the Civil Liability Act 1936`,
        `the item` `the claimant's driver's licence number (reg 5(2)(d))` `if` `the claimant was driving a motor vehicle when injured`,
        `the item` `proof of the claimant's identity (reg 5(2)(e))` `if` (NOT `the claimant was driving a motor vehicle when injured`),
        (LIST `a statement in the CTP Regulator's form authorising access to records and other information relevant to the claim (s 126A(2)(d), reg 6)`))

-- s 126A(6): "If a person fails to comply with a preceding subsection -- (a) the insurer or the nominal defendant ... may
-- decline to consider or deal with the claim while the failure continues; and (b) the person is not entitled, until the
-- person complies with the relevant requirements, to commence proceedings or to continue proceedings". Reg 4(2): a
-- failure to give the notice in time "does not attract the operation of section 126A(6)" if it "was occasioned by -- (i)
-- ignorance or mistake of the claimant; or (ii) absence of the claimant from the State; or (iii) inability of the
-- claimant on account of injury; or (iv) inability ... on account of the claimant's legal disability; or (v) other
-- reasonable cause; and (b) the proper assessment of the claim has not been substantially prejudiced." Reg 4(3):
-- "Nothing in section 126A ... affects the ability of a person to commence or continue proceedings once the person has
-- provided the information and statements referred to in subsection (2)(c) and (d) of that section (including after the
-- period ... has expired)." Finding 2: for a late notice, "while the failure continues" has no end the text names.
DECLARE `Cause of lateness` IS ONE OF
    `ignorance or mistake of the claimant`
    `absence of the claimant from the State`
    `inability of the claimant on account of injury`
    `inability of the claimant on account of legal disability`
    `other reasonable cause`
    `no such cause`

GIVEN `the cause` IS A `Cause of lateness`, `the assessment has been substantially prejudiced` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the lateness is excused (reg 4(2))` `the cause` `the assessment has been substantially prejudiced` IF
        (NOT (`the cause` EQUALS `no such cause`))
    AND (NOT `the assessment has been substantially prejudiced`)

DECLARE `Late notice answer` IS ONE OF
    `the notice was in time: s 126A(6) is not attracted by its timing`
    `late, but excused by reg 4(2): s 126A(6) is not attracted`
    `late and not excused: the insurer or nominal defendant may decline to consider or deal with the claim while the failure continues (s 126A(6)(a)), but proceedings may still be started or continued once the s 126A(2)(c) and (d) material is provided (reg 4(3))`

DECLARE `Proceedings answer` IS ONE OF
    `yes: section 126A does not stop the proceedings, because the s 126A(2)(c) and (d) material has been provided (s 126A(6)(b), reg 4(3))`
    `not yet: not until the s 126A(2)(c) and (d) material is provided (s 126A(6)(b))`

GIVEN `the (2)(c) and (d) material has been provided` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A `Proceedings answer`
`may proceedings be started or continued (s 126A(6)(b), reg 4(3))` `the (2)(c) and (d) material has been provided` MEANS
    IF `the (2)(c) and (d) material has been provided`
    THEN `yes: section 126A does not stop the proceedings, because the s 126A(2)(c) and (d) material has been provided (s 126A(6)(b), reg 4(3))`
    ELSE `not yet: not until the s 126A(2)(c) and (d) material is provided (s 126A(6)(b))`

-- s 126A(4): "An insurer or the nominal defendant must, within 21 days of receiving any record or other information under
-- subsection (2)(d), send a copy of the report or information to the claimant (or a legal practitioner engaged by the
-- claimant)."
`the days to send the claimant a copy (s 126A(4))` MEANS 21

§§ `s 115(3), (4), s 116(4), (5) -- notice to the nominal defendant, and dismissal for prejudice`

-- s 115(3), s 116(4): the claimant "must, as soon as reasonably practicable after it becomes apparent that the identity of
-- the vehicle is not readily ascertainable [the motor vehicle ... was uninsured], give to the nominal defendant notice in
-- writing of the person's claim and a short statement of the grounds". (4), (5): where the claimant fails, "and the court
-- ... is satisfied on the balance of probabilities that the defendant has been prejudiced in the conduct of the
-- defendant's defence by that failure, it may, if the justice of the case so requires, dismiss the action". Every
-- judgment here is the court's: inputs.
DECLARE `Dismissal answer` IS ONE OF
    `the action cannot be dismissed on this ground: the notice was given as soon as reasonably practicable`
    `the action cannot be dismissed on this ground: the court is not satisfied the nominal defendant was prejudiced`
    `the court may dismiss the action, if the justice of the case so requires (s 115(4), s 116(5))`

GIVEN `the notice was given as soon as reasonably practicable` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the court is satisfied the nominal defendant was prejudiced in its defence by the failure` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A `Dismissal answer`
`may the action against the nominal defendant be dismissed for late notice (s 115(4), s 116(5))` `the notice was given as soon as reasonably practicable` `the court is satisfied the nominal defendant was prejudiced in its defence by the failure` MEANS
    IF `the notice was given as soon as reasonably practicable`
    THEN `the action cannot be dismissed on this ground: the notice was given as soon as reasonably practicable`
    ELSE IF `the court is satisfied the nominal defendant was prejudiced in its defence by the failure`
         THEN `the court may dismiss the action, if the justice of the case so requires (s 115(4), s 116(5))`
         ELSE `the action cannot be dismissed on this ground: the court is not satisfied the nominal defendant was prejudiced`

§§ `ss 112, 113, 113A, 114, 117 -- recovering from the insurer directly`

-- s 112: where "(a) a person has obtained judgment in an action against an insured person for death or bodily injury
-- caused by, or arising out of the use of, an insured motor vehicle; and (b) before the action came on for hearing the
-- insurer knew that the action had been commenced, the judgment creditor may recover by action from the insurer such
-- amount of the money (including costs or a proportionate part of those costs) payable pursuant to the judgment as
-- relates to death or bodily injury and has not been paid."
-- s 113(1): where an insured person "who is wholly or partly liable for the death or bodily injury is dead or cannot be
-- served with process", and the claimant "has given notice of a claim under this section and a short statement of the
-- grounds of the claim as soon as possible after ascertaining that the insured person was dead or could not be found, or
-- within such time as would prevent the possibility of the insurer being prejudiced by want of such notice", the claimant
-- "may recover the amount of that judgment by action against the insurer". s 117: "a person who could have obtained a
-- judgment" includes a tortfeasor entitled to contribution (not encoded further).
-- s 114: "It is not a defence to an action under section 112 or 113 that the insurer is not liable under a policy of
-- insurance by reason of the fact that -- (a) the policy was obtained by mis-statement or non-disclosure; or (b) the
-- insured person has committed a breach of or failed to comply with a term, condition or warranty of the policy or a
-- provision of this Part."
DECLARE `Direct action situation` IS ONE OF
    `I have a judgment against the insured person that has not been paid`
    `the insured person who is liable is dead or cannot be served with process`
    `neither`

DECLARE `Direct action answer` IS ONE OF
    `yes: you may recover from the insurer the unpaid part of the judgment that relates to the death or bodily injury, with costs or a proportionate part of them (s 112)`
    `no: s 112 needs the insurer to have known of the action before it came on for hearing`
    `yes: you may recover by action against the insurer the amount of the judgment you could have obtained against the insured person (s 113)`
    `no: s 113 needs your notice and short statement of grounds given as soon as possible, or in time to prevent prejudice to the insurer`
    `the Act gives no direct action against the insurer in this situation: the claim is made against the insured person, which the insurer conducts (s 125)`

GIVEN `the situation` IS A `Direct action situation`
      `the insurer knew of the action before it came on for hearing` IS A BOOLEAN
      `I gave the s 113 notice in time` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A `Direct action answer`
`may the claimant recover from the insurer directly (ss 112, 113)` `the situation` `the insurer knew of the action before it came on for hearing` `I gave the s 113 notice in time` MEANS
    CONSIDER `the situation`
    WHEN `I have a judgment against the insured person that has not been paid` THEN
        IF `the insurer knew of the action before it came on for hearing`
        THEN `yes: you may recover from the insurer the unpaid part of the judgment that relates to the death or bodily injury, with costs or a proportionate part of them (s 112)`
        ELSE `no: s 112 needs the insurer to have known of the action before it came on for hearing`
    WHEN `the insured person who is liable is dead or cannot be served with process` THEN
        IF `I gave the s 113 notice in time`
        THEN `yes: you may recover by action against the insurer the amount of the judgment you could have obtained against the insured person (s 113)`
        ELSE `no: s 113 needs your notice and short statement of grounds given as soon as possible, or in time to prevent prejudice to the insurer`
    WHEN `neither` THEN `the Act gives no direct action against the insurer in this situation: the claim is made against the insured person, which the insurer conducts (s 125)`

DECLARE `Insurer's ground` IS ONE OF
    `the policy was obtained by mis-statement or non-disclosure`
    `the insured person broke a term, condition or warranty of the policy or a provision of Part 4`
    `some other ground`

DECLARE `Section 114 answer` IS ONE OF
    `not a defence to an action under s 112 or s 113 (s 114)`
    `s 114 does not deal with this ground`

GIVEN `the ground` IS A `Insurer's ground`
GIVETH A `Section 114 answer`
`is it a defence to a direct action (s 114)` `the ground` MEANS
    IF `the ground` EQUALS `some other ground`
    THEN `s 114 does not deal with this ground`
    ELSE `not a defence to an action under s 112 or s 113 (s 114)`

-- s 113A: "An insurer is not liable to pay any aggravated damages or exemplary or punitive damages awarded in an action
-- against the insured person ... and the insured person is not entitled to be indemnified by the insurer in respect of
-- such an award."
DECLARE `Kind of damages` IS ONE OF
    `compensatory damages for the death or bodily injury`
    `aggravated damages`
    `exemplary or punitive damages`

GIVEN `the kind` IS A `Kind of damages`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the insurer is barred from paying it (s 113A)` `the kind` IF
    NOT (`the kind` EQUALS `compensatory damages for the death or bodily injury`)

§§ `s 124AC -- credit for expenses the insurer has paid`

-- s 124AC: "If an amount claimed as expenses incurred as a result of death or bodily injury ... is paid by an insurer to
-- or on behalf of the claimant, the amount of any damages payable to the claimant in respect of the death or bodily
-- injury is reduced by the amount so paid." Fork F5: not below nil.
GIVEN `the damages` IS A NUMBER, `the expenses the insurer paid` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A NUMBER
`the damages after the s 124AC credit` `the damages` `the expenses the insurer paid` MEANS
    `the larger of` 0 `and` (`the damages` MINUS `the expenses the insurer paid`)

§§ `s 127(2), (6), (6a) -- the claimant's medical information duties`

-- s 127(2): "A claimant must -- (b) within 21 days of consulting a legally qualified medical practitioner in relation to
-- the injury ..., or such longer period as may be reasonable in the circumstances of the case or as the insurer may
-- allow, inform the insurer, by notice in writing, of -- (i) the name of the medical practitioner; and (ii) the day on
-- which the consultation occurred; and (c) within 21 days of receiving ... a written report from a legally qualified
-- medical practitioner consulted by the claimant in relation to the injury, or such longer period as may be reasonable
-- in the circumstances of the case, send a copy of that report to the insurer." The longer period is a judgment (or the
-- insurer's grant): an input of the extra days.
`the days for the medical notices (s 127(2)(b), (c))` MEANS 21

-- s 127(6): "If a claimant fails to comply with subsection (2)(b) or (c) and proceedings have been commenced before a
-- court -- (a) the court may award costs against the claimant; and (b) the court may take that failure into account in
-- assessing an award of compensation". (6a): not for a failure under (2)(c) where "the claimant has dealt with the medical
-- report and taken other action in accordance with any rules of the court" relieving a party from disclosing a report
-- that "would unfairly prejudice the party's case".
DECLARE `Medical duty` IS ONE OF
    `informing the insurer of a consultation (s 127(2)(b))`
    `sending the insurer a copy of a medical report (s 127(2)(c))`

DECLARE `Medical failure answer` IS ONE OF
    `no consequence under s 127(6): the duty was kept`
    `no consequence under s 127(6) yet: it applies once proceedings have been commenced before a court`
    `no consequence under s 127(6): the report was dealt with under the court's rules on prejudicial reports (s 127(6a))`
    `the court may award costs against you and may take the failure into account in assessing your compensation (s 127(6), (7))`

GIVEN `the duty` IS A `Medical duty`
      `the duty was kept` IS A BOOLEAN
      `proceedings have been commenced` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the report was dealt with under the court's rules on prejudicial reports` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A `Medical failure answer`
`what follows a failure of a s 127(2) duty` `the duty` `the duty was kept` `proceedings have been commenced` `the report was dealt with under the court's rules on prejudicial reports` MEANS
    IF `the duty was kept`
    THEN `no consequence under s 127(6): the duty was kept`
    ELSE IF NOT `proceedings have been commenced`
         THEN `no consequence under s 127(6) yet: it applies once proceedings have been commenced before a court`
         ELSE IF `the duty` EQUALS `sending the insurer a copy of a medical report (s 127(2)(c))` AND `the report was dealt with under the court's rules on prejudicial reports`
              THEN `no consequence under s 127(6): the report was dealt with under the court's rules on prejudicial reports (s 127(6a))`
              ELSE `the court may award costs against you and may take the failure into account in assessing your compensation (s 127(6), (7))`

§§ `s 127B -- treatment, care and support for a child under 16`

-- s 127B(2): "if -- (a) a person who has not attained the age of 16 years suffers bodily injury caused by or arising out of
-- the use of a motor vehicle; and (b) the incident to which the bodily injury is attributable occurs in South Australia,
-- then the insurer is liable to pay all the necessary and reasonable expenses with respect to the treatment, care and
-- support needs of the person (including after the person attains the age of 16 years) that are attributable to that
-- bodily injury." (3): "whether or not a liability at common law exists". (4): "This section does not apply in relation
-- to a participant in the Scheme under the Motor Vehicle Accidents (Lifetime Support Scheme) Act 2013." s 118B(2)(e)
-- applies s 127B to a claim against the nominal defendant. Sch 2 cl 22(4) (2013 Act): accidents from 1 July 2013.
-- Fork F6: the person's age is taken on the day of the accident. The treatment, care and support needs are (1)(a)-(g):
-- medical treatment (including pharmaceuticals), dental treatment, rehabilitation, ambulance transportation, aids and
-- appliances, prostheses, and other kinds the regulations prescribe (none in the deposited Regulations).
DECLARE `Section 127B answer` IS ONE OF
    `yes: the insurer must pay all the necessary and reasonable expenses of the child's treatment, care and support needs from the injury, even after 16, whether or not anyone was at fault (s 127B(2), (3))`
    `no: the child had turned 16 (s 127B(2)(a))`
    `no: the incident did not occur in South Australia (s 127B(2)(b))`
    `no: the child is a participant in the Lifetime Support Scheme, which deals with those needs instead (s 127B(4))`
    `no: the injury was not caused by and did not arise out of the use of a motor vehicle (s 127B(2)(a))`
    `no: there is no CTP insurer or nominal defendant for the claim under this Part`
    `not covered: an accident before 1 July 2013`

GIVEN `the age on the day of the accident` IS A NUMBER
      `the incident occurred in South Australia` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the child is a Lifetime Support Scheme participant` IS A BOOLEAN
      w IS A `Who answers the claim`
      `the accident day` IS A DATE
GIVETH A `Section 127B answer`
`must the insurer pay the child's treatment, care and support (s 127B)` `the age on the day of the accident` `the incident occurred in South Australia` `the child is a Lifetime Support Scheme participant` w `the accident day` MEANS
    IF NOT (`the accident is within this encoding` `the accident day`)
    THEN `not covered: an accident before 1 July 2013`
    ELSE IF w EQUALS `no claim under this Part: the death or injury was not caused by and did not arise out of the use of a motor vehicle (s 99)`
         THEN `no: the injury was not caused by and did not arise out of the use of a motor vehicle (s 127B(2)(a))`
         ELSE IF NOT (`there is a CTP claim under this Part` w)
              THEN `no: there is no CTP insurer or nominal defendant for the claim under this Part`
              ELSE IF `the age on the day of the accident` AT LEAST 16
                   THEN `no: the child had turned 16 (s 127B(2)(a))`
                   ELSE IF NOT `the incident occurred in South Australia`
                        THEN `no: the incident did not occur in South Australia (s 127B(2)(b))`
                        ELSE IF `the child is a Lifetime Support Scheme participant`
                             THEN `no: the child is a participant in the Lifetime Support Scheme, which deals with those needs instead (s 127B(4))`
                             ELSE `yes: the insurer must pay all the necessary and reasonable expenses of the child's treatment, care and support needs from the injury, even after 16, whether or not anyone was at fault (s 127B(2), (3))`

§§ `s 127C -- legal costs`

-- s 127C(1): costs "in relation to the claimant": "(a) if the total amount recovered in respect of the claim does not
-- exceed $25 000 -- the court must not award in favour of the claimant costs as between party and party unless the court
-- is of the opinion that there are exceptional circumstances justifying the award of such costs; (b) if the total amount
-- recovered ... exceeds $25 000 but does not exceed $100 000 -- the court must not award in favour of the claimant costs
-- ... in excess of the costs applying under the designated scale unless the court is satisfied that there are
-- exceptional circumstances". (2): the same for "the defendant", by "the total amount claimed". (3): (1)(a) does not
-- prevent party and party costs "insofar as the costs are directly related to obtaining" the court's approval of a
-- compromise or settlement for "a person who is under a legal disability". (4): "designated scale means the scale of
-- costs that apply in relation to civil proceedings in the Magistrates Court" (not deposited). Sch 2 cl 22(5) (2013 Act):
-- accidents from 1 July 2013.
`the lower costs threshold (s 127C)` MEANS 25000
`the upper costs threshold (s 127C)` MEANS 100000

DECLARE `Costs answer` IS ONE OF
    `no party and party costs may be awarded in your favour (s 127C(1)(a))`
    `party and party costs may be awarded, because the court finds exceptional circumstances (s 127C(1)(a))`
    `party and party costs only so far as directly related to obtaining the court's approval of the settlement for a person under a legal disability (s 127C(1)(a), (3))`
    `costs may be awarded, but not above the Magistrates Court scale (s 127C(1)(b))`
    `costs above the Magistrates Court scale may be awarded, because the court finds exceptional circumstances (s 127C(1)(b))`
    `section 127C does not limit the costs: the amount is over $100 000`
    `no party and party costs may be awarded to the defendant (s 127C(2)(a))`
    `party and party costs may be awarded to the defendant, because the court finds exceptional circumstances (s 127C(2)(a))`
    `costs may be awarded to the defendant, but not above the Magistrates Court scale (s 127C(2)(b))`
    `costs above the Magistrates Court scale may be awarded to the defendant, because the court finds exceptional circumstances (s 127C(2)(b))`
    `not covered: an accident before 1 July 2013`

GIVEN `the total amount recovered` IS A NUMBER
      `the court finds exceptional circumstances` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the proceedings are to approve a settlement for a person under a legal disability` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the accident day` IS A DATE
GIVETH A `Costs answer`
`the claimant's costs (s 127C(1), (3))` `the total amount recovered` `the court finds exceptional circumstances` `the proceedings are to approve a settlement for a person under a legal disability` `the accident day` MEANS
    IF NOT (`the accident is within this encoding` `the accident day`)
    THEN `not covered: an accident before 1 July 2013`
    ELSE IF `the total amount recovered` AT MOST `the lower costs threshold (s 127C)`
         THEN IF `the court finds exceptional circumstances`
              THEN `party and party costs may be awarded, because the court finds exceptional circumstances (s 127C(1)(a))`
              ELSE IF `the proceedings are to approve a settlement for a person under a legal disability`
                   THEN `party and party costs only so far as directly related to obtaining the court's approval of the settlement for a person under a legal disability (s 127C(1)(a), (3))`
                   ELSE `no party and party costs may be awarded in your favour (s 127C(1)(a))`
         ELSE IF `the total amount recovered` AT MOST `the upper costs threshold (s 127C)`
              THEN IF `the court finds exceptional circumstances`
                   THEN `costs above the Magistrates Court scale may be awarded, because the court finds exceptional circumstances (s 127C(1)(b))`
                   ELSE `costs may be awarded, but not above the Magistrates Court scale (s 127C(1)(b))`
              ELSE `section 127C does not limit the costs: the amount is over $100 000`

GIVEN `the total amount claimed` IS A NUMBER
      `the court finds exceptional circumstances` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the accident day` IS A DATE
GIVETH A `Costs answer`
`the defendant's costs (s 127C(2))` `the total amount claimed` `the court finds exceptional circumstances` `the accident day` MEANS
    IF NOT (`the accident is within this encoding` `the accident day`)
    THEN `not covered: an accident before 1 July 2013`
    ELSE IF `the total amount claimed` AT MOST `the lower costs threshold (s 127C)`
         THEN IF `the court finds exceptional circumstances`
              THEN `party and party costs may be awarded to the defendant, because the court finds exceptional circumstances (s 127C(2)(a))`
              ELSE `no party and party costs may be awarded to the defendant (s 127C(2)(a))`
         ELSE IF `the total amount claimed` AT MOST `the upper costs threshold (s 127C)`
              THEN IF `the court finds exceptional circumstances`
                   THEN `costs above the Magistrates Court scale may be awarded to the defendant, because the court finds exceptional circumstances (s 127C(2)(b))`
                   ELSE `costs may be awarded to the defendant, but not above the Magistrates Court scale (s 127C(2)(b))`
              ELSE `section 127C does not limit the costs: the amount is over $100 000`

§§ `s 133 -- contracting out`

-- s 133(1): "any contract ... by virtue of which a person contracts in advance out of any right to claim damages or any
-- other remedy in respect of death or bodily injury caused by or arising out of the use of a motor vehicle is to that
-- extent void." (2): it does not apply "to a right or remedy in respect of loss of or damage to property". Fork F7: "in
-- advance" read as made before the death or injury; an agreement after it (such as a settlement) is outside s 133.
DECLARE `What the right is about` IS ONE OF
    `death or bodily injury`
    `loss of or damage to property`

DECLARE `Contracting out answer` IS ONE OF
    `void to that extent: you keep your right to claim (s 133(1))`
    `section 133 does not apply to property claims (s 133(2))`
    `section 133 does not deal with an agreement made after the death or injury`

GIVEN `the right` IS A `What the right is about`, `the agreement was made before the death or injury` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A `Contracting out answer`
`is the agreement giving up the right void (s 133)` `the right` `the agreement was made before the death or injury` MEANS
    IF `the right` EQUALS `loss of or damage to property`
    THEN `section 133 does not apply to property claims (s 133(2))`
    ELSE IF `the agreement was made before the death or injury`
         THEN `void to that extent: you keep your right to claim (s 133(1))`
         ELSE `section 133 does not deal with an agreement made after the death or injury`

§§ `s 134 -- who may act on a claim for reward`

-- s 134(1): a person must not, "for or in the expectation of directly or indirectly receiving any reward", accept
-- instructions to act, act, or hold themself out as willing to act, on a claim for damages arising out of the use of a
-- motor vehicle. Maximum penalty: $750. (2): it "does not apply to -- (a) any qualified legal practitioner acting in the
-- course of the legal practitioner's profession; (b) any officer of an association formed for the protection of the
-- interests of employees ... when acting in the course of the officer's duties for a member of that association; (c) any
-- person employed or instructed by an approved insurer" (on the insurer's side). (3): "An agreement to pay money for work
-- done or services rendered contrary to this section is void and any money so paid is recoverable by the person who has
-- paid it."
DECLARE `Who would act` IS ONE OF
    `a qualified legal practitioner acting in the course of their profession`
    `an officer of an employees' association acting for me as a member`
    `a person employed or instructed by an approved insurer`
    `anyone else`

DECLARE `Acting for reward answer` IS ONE OF
    `they may act for reward (s 134(2))`
    `they must not act for reward (maximum penalty $750); an agreement to pay them is void and money paid is recoverable (s 134(1), (3))`
    `section 134 does not apply: no reward is given or expected`

GIVEN `who` IS A `Who would act`, `for reward` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A `Acting for reward answer`
`may this person act on the claim for reward (s 134)` `who` `for reward` MEANS
    IF NOT `for reward`
    THEN `section 134 does not apply: no reward is given or expected`
    ELSE IF `who` EQUALS `anyone else`
         THEN `they must not act for reward (maximum penalty $750); an agreement to pay them is void and money paid is recoverable (s 134(1), (3))`
         ELSE `they may act for reward (s 134(2))`

`the maximum penalty for acting for reward (s 134(1))` MEANS 750

§§ `s 124(6), (6a), (6b) -- false or misleading information`

-- s 124(6): no notice or information under s 124 "that is, to the person's knowledge, false or misleading in any material
-- particular"; (6a): no information to the insurer (or someone known to be engaged by it) "that the person knows is
-- material to the claim and is false or misleading". Maximum penalty for each: "$50 000 or imprisonment for one year".
-- (6b): if an amount has been paid to the claimant and the claimant "has been found guilty of an offence against
-- subsection (6) or (6a) in connection with the claim, the person who made the payment is entitled to recover from the
-- claimant the amount of any financial benefit that the claimant gained from the commission of the offence together with
-- any amount that the court considers appropriate in respect of costs".
`the maximum fine for false information (s 124(6), (6a))` MEANS 50000
`the maximum imprisonment in years for false information (s 124(6), (6a))` MEANS 1

DECLARE `False information recovery answer` IS ONE OF
    `the payer may recover from you the financial benefit you gained, plus the costs the court considers appropriate (s 124(6b))` HAS `the financial benefit` IS A NUMBER
    `no recovery under s 124(6b): it needs a payment to you and a finding of guilt under s 124(6) or (6a)`

GIVEN `an amount has been paid to me` IS A BOOLEAN
      `I have been found guilty under s 124(6) or (6a)` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the financial benefit I gained from the offence` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A `False information recovery answer`
`what can be recovered for false information (s 124(6b))` `an amount has been paid to me` `I have been found guilty under s 124(6) or (6a)` `the financial benefit I gained from the offence` MEANS
    IF `an amount has been paid to me` AND `I have been found guilty under s 124(6) or (6a)`
    THEN `the payer may recover from you the financial benefit you gained, plus the costs the court considers appropriate (s 124(6b))` `the financial benefit I gained from the offence`
    ELSE `no recovery under s 124(6b): it needs a payment to you and a finding of guilt under s 124(6) or (6a)`

§§ `s 124(1)-(4), s 126 -- the owner's, person in charge's and driver's duties`

-- s 124(1): after an accident causing death or bodily injury, "written notice must be given as soon as practicable to the
-- person who is the insurer" stating "(a) the fact of the accident; (b) the time and place at which it occurred; (c) the
-- circumstances of the accident; (ca) the name, date of birth and address of the driver ...; (d) the name and address of
-- any person killed or injured ...; (e) the names and addresses of any witnesses". (2): if not given, "the owner, the
-- person in charge, and the driver ... are each guilty of an offence. Maximum penalty: $1 250 or imprisonment for 3
-- months." (3) defences: "(a) that the defendant did not know of the accident, or gave the requisite notice as soon as
-- practicable after the accident came to the defendant's knowledge; or (b) that the defendant believed upon reasonable
-- grounds that the requisite notice had been given; or (c)" for a missing detail, reasonable inquiries and compliance to
-- the best of the defendant's knowledge. (3a): the owner, person in charge or driver "must co-operate fully with the
-- insurer in respect of a claim". Maximum penalty: $5 000. (4): "Where a claim is made upon an insured person ..., the
-- insured person must as soon as practicable give notice of the claim to the insurer and furnish the insurer with such
-- information ... as the insurer may reasonably require. Maximum penalty: $750."
DECLARE `Accident notice detail` IS ONE OF
    `the fact of the accident`
    `the time and place at which it occurred`
    `the circumstances of the accident`
    `the name, date of birth and address of the driver at the time`
    `the name and address of any person killed or injured`
    `the names and addresses of any witnesses`

`the details of the accident notice (s 124(1))` MEANS
    LIST `the fact of the accident`,
         `the time and place at which it occurred`,
         `the circumstances of the accident`,
         `the name, date of birth and address of the driver at the time`,
         `the name and address of any person killed or injured`,
         `the names and addresses of any witnesses`

`the maximum fine for no accident notice (s 124(2))` MEANS 1250
`the maximum imprisonment in months for no accident notice (s 124(2))` MEANS 3
`the maximum fine for not co-operating (s 124(3a))` MEANS 5000
`the maximum fine for not notifying the insurer of a claim (s 124(4))` MEANS 750

DECLARE `Accident notice defence` IS ONE OF
    `I did not know of the accident`
    `I gave the notice as soon as practicable after I learned of the accident`
    `I believed on reasonable grounds that the notice had been given`
    `a detail was missing, but I made reasonable inquiries and complied to the best of my knowledge`
    `none of these`

DECLARE `Accident notice answer` IS ONE OF
    `the notice was given as soon as practicable: no offence under s 124(2)`
    `you have a defence under s 124(3)`
    `you may be guilty of an offence: maximum penalty $1 250 or imprisonment for 3 months (s 124(2))`
    `section 124 does not apply: the accident caused no death or bodily injury`

GIVEN `the accident caused death or bodily injury` IS A BOOLEAN
      `written notice was given as soon as practicable` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the defence` IS A `Accident notice defence`
GIVETH A `Accident notice answer`
`the accident notice duty (s 124(1)-(3))` `the accident caused death or bodily injury` `written notice was given as soon as practicable` `the defence` MEANS
    IF NOT `the accident caused death or bodily injury`
    THEN `section 124 does not apply: the accident caused no death or bodily injury`
    ELSE IF `written notice was given as soon as practicable`
         THEN `the notice was given as soon as practicable: no offence under s 124(2)`
         ELSE IF `the defence` EQUALS `none of these`
              THEN `you may be guilty of an offence: maximum penalty $1 250 or imprisonment for 3 months (s 124(2))`
              ELSE `you have a defence under s 124(3)`

-- s 126: "An insured person must not, without the consent in writing of the insurer concerned -- (a) enter upon, or
-- incur any expense in, any litigation; or (b) make any offer or promise of payment or settlement; or (c) make any
-- payment or settlement; or (d) make any admission of liability; or (e) authorise the repair of the motor vehicle, or
-- dismantle, or wilfully cause damage to, the motor vehicle, in respect of any claim ..., but this provision does not
-- prevent any person from truthfully answering any question reasonably asked of the person by any police officer." A
-- breach is a s 124A(2)(b)(ii) ground of recovery.
DECLARE `Insured person's step` IS ONE OF
    `start or spend money on litigation`
    `offer or promise a payment or settlement`
    `make a payment or settlement`
    `admit liability`
    `authorise repairs to, dismantle or wilfully damage the vehicle`
    `truthfully answer a question a police officer reasonably asks`
    `something else`

DECLARE `Step answer` IS ONE OF
    `not without the insurer's written consent (s 126)`
    `yes: the insurer has consented in writing (s 126)`
    `yes: s 126 does not stop you truthfully answering a police officer's reasonable question`
    `section 126 does not deal with this step`

GIVEN `the step` IS A `Insured person's step`, `the insurer has consented in writing` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A `Step answer`
`may the insured person take this step (s 126)` `the step` `the insurer has consented in writing` MEANS
    CONSIDER `the step`
    WHEN `truthfully answer a question a police officer reasonably asks` THEN `yes: s 126 does not stop you truthfully answering a police officer's reasonable question`
    WHEN `something else` THEN `section 126 does not deal with this step`
    OTHERWISE
        IF `the insurer has consented in writing`
        THEN `yes: the insurer has consented in writing (s 126)`
        ELSE `not without the insurer's written consent (s 126)`

§§ `ss 123, 124A -- what the insurer may recover from the insured person`

-- s 124A(1): the insurer "may ... recover from the insured person any money paid or costs incurred" where the insured
-- person contravened a term of the policy "(aa) by driving ... with the intention of causing the death of, or bodily
-- injury to, a person or damage to another's property, or with reckless indifference ...; or (a) by driving ... while so
-- much under the influence of intoxicating liquor or a drug as to be incapable of exercising effective control of the
-- vehicle; or (b) by driving ... while there is present in the insured person's blood a concentration of .1 grams or more
-- of alcohol in 100 millilitres of blood". (2): "to the prejudice of the insurer", for "(aa) ... committing an offence
-- against section 43 of the Road Traffic Act 1961" [duty to stop and give assistance after an accident], "(a) ... any
-- other term of the policy", or "(b) ... a requirement of section 124; or ... a provision of section 126", the court
-- fixes "so much ... as the court thinks just and reasonable". (2b): the s 43 offence counts "if, and only if, the person
-- has been found guilty". (3): for a road race participant, recovery is "from the organiser of the road race". s 123:
-- against a person who "without lawful excuse drives a motor vehicle without first obtaining the consent of its owner",
-- "any money paid or costs incurred". The terms of the policy are the CTP Regulator's (not deposited): ground (2)(a) is
-- asked as a fact.
`the blood alcohol concentration for full recovery (s 124A(1)(b), s 116(7)(c)(iii))` MEANS 0.1

DECLARE `Insured's conduct` HAS
    `I drove intending death, injury or property damage, or with reckless indifference`  IS A BOOLEAN
    `I was so affected by alcohol or a drug that I could not control the vehicle`         IS A BOOLEAN
    `my blood alcohol concentration in grams per 100 millilitres`                       IS A NUMBER
    `I was found guilty of an offence against s 43 of the Road Traffic Act 1961`        IS A BOOLEAN
    `I broke another term of the policy`                                                 IS A BOOLEAN
    `I failed to comply with s 124 or s 126`                                             IS A BOOLEAN
    `the insurer was prejudiced`                                                          IS A BOOLEAN
    `I was a participant in a road race`                                                  IS A BOOLEAN
    `I drove without the owner's consent and without lawful excuse`                      IS A BOOLEAN

DECLARE `Insurer recovery answer` IS ONE OF
    `the insurer may recover all the money it paid and costs it incurred (s 123)`
    `the insurer may recover all the money it paid and costs it incurred (s 124A(1))`
    `the insurer may recover as much as the court thinks just and reasonable (s 124A(2), (4))`
    `the insurer recovers from the organiser of the road race, not under s 124A from you (s 124A(3))`
    `no recovery under s 123 or s 124A on these facts`

GIVEN c IS AN `Insured's conduct`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `a s 124A(1) ground is made out` c IF
       c's `I drove intending death, injury or property damage, or with reckless indifference`
    OR c's `I was so affected by alcohol or a drug that I could not control the vehicle`
    OR c's `my blood alcohol concentration in grams per 100 millilitres` AT LEAST `the blood alcohol concentration for full recovery (s 124A(1)(b), s 116(7)(c)(iii))`

GIVEN c IS AN `Insured's conduct`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `a s 124A(2) ground is made out` c IF
        c's `the insurer was prejudiced`
    AND (   c's `I was found guilty of an offence against s 43 of the Road Traffic Act 1961`
         OR c's `I broke another term of the policy`
         OR c's `I failed to comply with s 124 or s 126`)

-- Fork F8: where several grounds hold, the widest recovery is named (s 123 and s 124A(1) before s 124A(2)); s 124A(3)
-- is named only where no ground against the insured person is made out.
GIVEN c IS AN `Insured's conduct`
GIVETH A `Insurer recovery answer`
`what may the insurer recover from the insured person (ss 123, 124A)` c MEANS
    IF c's `I drove without the owner's consent and without lawful excuse`
    THEN `the insurer may recover all the money it paid and costs it incurred (s 123)`
    ELSE IF `a s 124A(1) ground is made out` c
         THEN `the insurer may recover all the money it paid and costs it incurred (s 124A(1))`
         ELSE IF `a s 124A(2) ground is made out` c
              THEN `the insurer may recover as much as the court thinks just and reasonable (s 124A(2), (4))`
              ELSE IF c's `I was a participant in a road race`
                   THEN `the insurer recovers from the organiser of the road race, not under s 124A from you (s 124A(3))`
                   ELSE `no recovery under s 123 or s 124A on these facts`

§§ `s 124AB -- recovery of an excess`

-- s 124AB(1): where the liability "arises out of an accident which was to the extent of more than 25 per cent the fault of
-- the insured person, the insurer may recover from the insured person as a debt -- (a) if the money paid and costs
-- incurred ... do not exceed the prescribed amount -- (i) the amount of that money paid and costs incurred; or (ii) if
-- the amount to be recovered ... is received within 1 month following a first request for payment -- 95% of [it]; and (b)
-- if [they] exceed the prescribed amount -- (i) the prescribed amount; or (ii) if ... received within 1 month following a
-- first request for payment -- 95% of the prescribed amount." (2): not "if the insurer exercises any other right of
-- recovery against the insured person under this Part". (3): "the prescribed amount is $460". (4): "adjusted on 1 January
-- of each year, beginning on 1 January 2012, by multiplying the stated amount by a proportion obtained by dividing the CPI
-- for the September quarter of the immediately preceding year by the CPI for the September quarter 2010 (with the amount
-- so adjusted being rounded up to the nearest multiple of $10)". (5): the prescribed amount "for the year in which the
-- accident occurred". "CPI" is defined in s 5 of the Act (the all groups index for Adelaide); the figures are inputs.
`the stated prescribed amount (s 124AB(3))` MEANS 460
`the fault share that must be exceeded (s 124AB(1))` MEANS 25
`the share payable if paid within 1 month (s 124AB(1)(a)(ii), (b)(ii))` MEANS 95%
`the months to pay for the reduced amount (s 124AB(1))` MEANS 1

GIVEN `the accident year` IS A NUMBER
      `the CPI for the September quarter of the year before the accident` IS A NUMBER
      `the CPI for the September quarter 2010` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A NUMBER
`the prescribed amount for the year of the accident (s 124AB(3)-(5))` `the accident year` `the CPI for the September quarter of the year before the accident` `the CPI for the September quarter 2010` MEANS
    IF `the accident year` LESS THAN 2012
    THEN `the stated prescribed amount (s 124AB(3))`
    ELSE `rounded up to the nearest multiple of $10` (`the stated prescribed amount (s 124AB(3))` TIMES (`the CPI for the September quarter of the year before the accident` DIVIDED BY `the CPI for the September quarter 2010`))

GIVEN `the money paid and costs incurred` IS A NUMBER, `the prescribed amount` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A NUMBER
`the full excess (s 124AB(1))` `the money paid and costs incurred` `the prescribed amount` MEANS
    `the smaller of` `the money paid and costs incurred` `and` `the prescribed amount`

§§ `s 116(7)-(7e) -- what the nominal defendant may recover from the driver of an uninsured vehicle`

-- s 116(7): where the nominal defendant properly paid a claim for an uninsured vehicle, the driver "was wholly or partly
-- liable", and the driver drove (i) intending death, injury or property damage or with reckless indifference, (ii) so
-- affected by liquor or a drug as to be incapable of effective control, or (iii) with ".1 grams or more of alcohol in 100
-- millilitres of blood", "the nominal defendant may ... recover that sum together with costs from the driver". (7aa): for
-- an offence against s 43 of the Road Traffic Act 1961 (found guilty: (7ab)), "that sum, or such part of that sum as the
-- court thinks just and reasonable". (7b): otherwise (the driver liable but none of (7)(c)), the same "just and
-- reasonable" recovery. (7c): "It is a defence to an action under subsection (7b) if the defendant proves that -- (a) the
-- motor vehicle was being used at the relevant time by or with the consent of the owner; and (b) the defendant did not
-- know and could not reasonably be expected to have known that the vehicle was an uninsured motor vehicle." (7d): not if
-- the driver "(a) drove the vehicle while not duly licensed or otherwise permitted by law to drive the vehicle; or (b)
-- drove the vehicle while the vehicle was overloaded, or in an unsafe, unroadworthy or damaged condition; or (c) committed
-- an offence against section 43". Finding 3: a s 43 case is in both (7aa) and (7b); (7d)(c) keeps the (7c) defence from
-- it either way.
DECLARE `Uninsured driver's facts` HAS
    `I was wholly or partly liable for the death or injury`                                IS A BOOLEAN
    `I drove intending death, injury or property damage, or with reckless indifference`  IS A BOOLEAN
    `I was so affected by alcohol or a drug that I could not control the vehicle`         IS A BOOLEAN
    `my blood alcohol concentration in grams per 100 millilitres`                       IS A NUMBER
    `I was found guilty of an offence against s 43 of the Road Traffic Act 1961`        IS A BOOLEAN
    `the vehicle was used by or with the owner's consent`                                 IS A BOOLEAN
    `I did not know and could not reasonably have known it was uninsured`                IS A BOOLEAN
    `I was not duly licensed or permitted to drive it`                                    IS A BOOLEAN
    `the vehicle was overloaded, unsafe, unroadworthy or damaged`                        IS A BOOLEAN

DECLARE `Nominal defendant recovery answer` IS ONE OF
    `the nominal defendant may recover the whole sum it paid, with costs (s 116(7))`
    `the nominal defendant may recover the sum, or the part the court thinks just and reasonable, with costs (s 116(7aa))`
    `the nominal defendant may recover the sum, or the part the court thinks just and reasonable, with costs (s 116(7b), (7e))`
    `you have a defence to recovery under s 116(7b) (s 116(7c))`
    `no recovery under s 116: you were not liable for the death or injury`

GIVEN d IS AN `Uninsured driver's facts`
GIVETH A `Nominal defendant recovery answer`
`what may the nominal defendant recover from the uninsured driver (s 116(7)-(7e))` d MEANS
    IF NOT (d's `I was wholly or partly liable for the death or injury`)
    THEN `no recovery under s 116: you were not liable for the death or injury`
    ELSE IF    d's `I drove intending death, injury or property damage, or with reckless indifference`
            OR d's `I was so affected by alcohol or a drug that I could not control the vehicle`
            OR d's `my blood alcohol concentration in grams per 100 millilitres` AT LEAST `the blood alcohol concentration for full recovery (s 124A(1)(b), s 116(7)(c)(iii))`
         THEN `the nominal defendant may recover the whole sum it paid, with costs (s 116(7))`
         ELSE IF d's `I was found guilty of an offence against s 43 of the Road Traffic Act 1961`
              THEN `the nominal defendant may recover the sum, or the part the court thinks just and reasonable, with costs (s 116(7aa))`
              ELSE IF     d's `the vehicle was used by or with the owner's consent`
                      AND d's `I did not know and could not reasonably have known it was uninsured`
                      AND (NOT d's `I was not duly licensed or permitted to drive it`)
                      AND (NOT d's `the vehicle was overloaded, unsafe, unroadworthy or damaged`)
                   THEN `you have a defence to recovery under s 116(7b) (s 116(7c))`
                   ELSE `the nominal defendant may recover the sum, or the part the court thinks just and reasonable, with costs (s 116(7b), (7e))`

§§ `The regulative rules`

DECLARE `CTP party` IS ONE OF
    `the claimant`
    `the insurer`
    `the nominal defendant`
    `the insured person`

DECLARE `CTP act` IS ONE OF
    `furnish a notice of claim complying with s 126A(2)`
    `inform the insurer in writing of the medical practitioner consulted and the day of the consultation`
    `send the insurer a copy of the medical report`
    `send the claimant a copy of the record or information`
    `pay the excess the insurer requests`

-- s 126A(1), (2)(a), reg 4(1)(b): the claimant must give the insurer (or, under s 118A, the nominal defendant) a
-- complying notice within 6 months after the accident. Clock: day 0 is the accident day; the seat works out the days to
-- the last day. Late without a reg 4(2) excuse: BREACH, which here means s 126A(6) is attracted.
GIVEN `the respondent` IS A `CTP party`, `the days allowed` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A DEONTIC `CTP party` `CTP act`
`s 126A(1), reg 4(1)(b) -- the claimant must furnish a complying notice of claim within 6 months after the accident` `the respondent` `the days allowed` MEANS
    PARTY  `the claimant`
    MUST   `furnish a notice of claim complying with s 126A(2)`
    WITHIN `the days allowed`
    LEST   BREACH BY `the claimant` BECAUSE "s 126A(2)(a), reg 4(1)(b): no complying notice of claim within 6 months after the accident"

-- s 127(2)(b): within 21 days of the consultation (or a longer period that is reasonable or the insurer allows: the extra
-- days are an input). Clock: day 0 is the consultation.
GIVEN `the days allowed` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A DEONTIC `CTP party` `CTP act`
`s 127(2)(b) -- the claimant must inform the insurer of a consultation within 21 days` `the days allowed` MEANS
    PARTY  `the claimant`
    MUST   `inform the insurer in writing of the medical practitioner consulted and the day of the consultation`
    WITHIN `the days allowed`
    LEST   BREACH BY `the claimant` BECAUSE "s 127(2)(b): the insurer was not told of the consultation within the time"

-- s 127(2)(c): within 21 days of receiving the written report (or a longer reasonable period). Clock: day 0 is receipt.
GIVEN `the days allowed` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A DEONTIC `CTP party` `CTP act`
`s 127(2)(c) -- the claimant must send the insurer a copy of a medical report within 21 days of receiving it` `the days allowed` MEANS
    PARTY  `the claimant`
    MUST   `send the insurer a copy of the medical report`
    WITHIN `the days allowed`
    LEST   BREACH BY `the claimant` BECAUSE "s 127(2)(c): no copy of the medical report sent to the insurer within the time"

-- s 126A(4): the insurer or nominal defendant must send the claimant a copy within 21 days. Clock: day 0 is receipt.
GIVEN `the respondent` IS A `CTP party`
GIVETH A DEONTIC `CTP party` `CTP act`
`s 126A(4) -- the insurer or nominal defendant must send the claimant a copy of each record it obtains within 21 days` `the respondent` MEANS
    PARTY  `the respondent`
    MUST   `send the claimant a copy of the record or information`
    WITHIN `the days to send the claimant a copy (s 126A(4))`
    LEST   BREACH BY `the respondent` BECAUSE "s 126A(4): no copy sent to the claimant within 21 days of receiving it"

-- s 124AB(1)(a)(ii), (b)(ii): paying within 1 month after the first request earns the 95% rate; otherwise the full
-- excess is a debt. Clock: day 0 is the first request. Paid in time: FULFILLED (95%). Not in time: the full debt remains.
GIVEN `the days allowed` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A DEONTIC `CTP party` `CTP act`
`s 124AB(1) -- the insured person may pay 95% of the excess within 1 month after the first request, otherwise the full excess` `the days allowed` MEANS
    PARTY  `the insured person`
    MUST   `pay the excess the insurer requests`
    WITHIN `the days allowed`
    LEST
        PARTY  `the insured person`
        MUST   `pay the excess the insurer requests`
        LEST   BREACH BY `the insured person` BECAUSE "s 124AB(1): the excess is a debt and remains unpaid"

-- The helper that runs a duty over what happened. On the stable l4 a CONSIDER on an EVALTRACE can match FULFILLED but not
-- BREACH; so where the act was done, FULFILLED means in time and anything else late; where it was not, the helper asks
-- whether doing it now would still be in time. It takes a DEONTIC value, so it is never exported.
DECLARE `What became of the duty` IS ONE OF
    `the duty was kept in time`
    `the duty was not kept in time`
    `the time has not yet run out`

GIVEN `the rule` IS A DEONTIC `CTP party` `CTP act`
      `the party` IS A `CTP party`
      `the act` IS A `CTP act`
      `the act has been done` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the time it was done` IS A NUMBER
      `the time now` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A `What became of the duty`
`what became of` `the rule` `for` `the party` `doing` `the act` `done` `the act has been done` `at` `the time it was done` `now` `the time now` MEANS
    IF `the act has been done`
    THEN CONSIDER EVALTRACE `the rule` 0 (LIST (EVENT `the party` `the act` `the time it was done`))
         WHEN FULFILLED THEN `the duty was kept in time`
         OTHERWISE `the duty was not kept in time`
    ELSE CONSIDER EVALTRACE `the rule` 0 (LIST (EVENT `the party` `the act` `the time now`))
         WHEN FULFILLED THEN `the time has not yet run out`
         OTHERWISE `the duty was not kept in time`
